(i) for $S$ to be a subspace of $M_{4 \times 4}(\mathbb{R})$, it should be closed under addition and multiplication
\[ S(\vec{y} + \vec{z}) = (\vec{y}_1 + \vec{z}_1) + (\vec{y}_2 + \vec{z}_2) + (\vec{y}_3 + \vec{z}_3) - (\vec{y}_4 + \vec{z}_4) \]
\[ + \vec{y}, \vec{z} \in M_{4 \times 4}(\mathbb{R}) = (\vec{y}_1 + \vec{y}_2 + \vec{y}_3 - \vec{y}_4) + (\vec{z}_1 + \vec{z}_2 + \vec{z}_3 - \vec{z}_4) \]
\[ S(\vec{y} + \vec{z}) = S(\vec{y}) + S(\vec{z}) \]
Hence, $S \subset M_{4 \times 4}(\mathbb{R})$ is closed under addition.

Now $S(t\vec{y}) = t\vec{y}_1 + t\vec{y}_2 + t\vec{y}_3 - t\vec{y}_4$
$\forall \vec{y} \in M_{4 \times 4}(\mathbb{R}) = t(\vec{y}_1 + \vec{y}_2 + \vec{y}_3 - \vec{y}_4)$
\[ S(t\vec{y}) = t S(\vec{y}) \]
Hence, $S \subset M_{4 \times 4}(\mathbb{R})$ is closed under multiplication

Hence, proved $S$ is a subspace of $M_{4 \times 4}(\mathbb{R})$.

