(i) For each $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$
\[ \frac{\hat{u} \cdot \hat{v}_i}{\|\hat{u}\| \|\hat{v}_i\|} = \cos \alpha_i \quad -(1) \quad [\|\hat{u}\| = 1 = \|\hat{v}_i\|] \]
Now since $\{\hat{v}_1, \dots, \hat{v}_n\}$ forms a basis for $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\hat{u}$ is a unit vector in $\mathbb{R}^n$ we can $\hat{u}$ as a linear combination of $\{\hat{v}_1, \dots, \hat{v}_n\}$ with coefficients $b_1, b_2, \dots, b_n \in \mathbb{R}$
\[ \hat{u} = b_1 \hat{v}_1 + \dots + b_n \hat{v}_n \]
Multiplying both side by $\hat{v}_1$
\[ \hat{u} \cdot \hat{v}_1 = b_1 \quad [\text{since } \hat{v}_1 \cdot \hat{v}_j = 0 \text{ when } i \neq j] \]
Now from (1) we can say $\boxed{b_1 = \cos \alpha_i}$
Similarly doing for $\{\hat{v}_2, \dots, \hat{v}_n\}$ we get
\[ \hat{u} = \cos \alpha_1 \hat{v}_1 + \cos \alpha_2 \hat{v}_2 + \dots + \cos \alpha_n \hat{v}_n \]
Taking Worm on both sides we get and then squaring:
\[ 1 = \frac{\cos^2 \alpha_1}{\cos \alpha_1} + \cos^2 \alpha_2 + \dots + \cos^2 \alpha_n \]
Using $\sin^2 \alpha_i = 1 - \cos^2 \alpha_i$
\[ 1 = 1 - \sin^2 \alpha_1 + 1 - \sin^2 \alpha_2 + \dots + 1 - \sin^2 \alpha_n \]
\[ 1 + \sin^2 \alpha_1 + \dots + \sin^2 \alpha_n = n \]
Hence proved.

