(b) If $\beta = \{b_1, b_2, \dots, b_n\}$ is a basis for $\mathbb{V}$, \\
then $(t_1 b_1 + t_2 b_2 + \dots + t_n b_n)$ can span the whole $\mathbb{V}$. \\
Any linear operations on $\beta$ will lead to $n$ vectors \\
which form the span of $\mathbb{V}$ space. \\
So, $f(\beta) = \{f(b_1), f(b_2), \dots, f(b_n)\}$ will be a \\
linearly operated form of the basis of $\mathbb{V}$ \\
Hence, \\
$f(\beta) = \{(f(b_1), f(b_2), \dots, f(b_n))\}$ will give the linear \\
combination as: \\
$(t_1 f(b_1) + t_2 f(b_2) + \dots + t_n f(b_n))$ \\
It can span the whole basis $\mathbb{V}$ and the number of elements \\
is same as $\beta$. \\
Hence $f(\beta)$ is a basis for $\mathbb{V}$.

