T.P.T: $\forall x_n \to x, f_n(x_n) \to f(x)$

Proof: let $(x_n)$ be a seq in $X$ s.t. $x_n \to x$ for $x \in X$

claim: $f_n(x_n) \to f(x)$

Since, $(x_n) \to x \therefore$ let $s, t \to 0$ be given then $\exists N \in \mathbb{N}$ s.t. every neighbourhood of $x$ contains $\infty$ many points

let $B_{s_1}(x)$ be nbd of $x$ s.t. $\forall n \geq N, x_n \in B_{s_1}(x) \Rightarrow x_n \in B(x, s_1)$

$\rightarrow (1)$

Since, $f_n \to f$ uniformly. let $\exists t \to 0$ be given $\exists N \in \mathbb{N}$ s.t. $d(f_n(x), f(x)) < \frac{\epsilon}{2} \quad \forall n \geq N, \rightarrow (2)$

$\therefore$ This holds true even when $x_n$ is a sequence i.e. $d(f_n(x_n), f(x_n)) < \frac{\epsilon}{2} \quad \forall n \geq N, \rightarrow (3)$

$\rightarrow$ Since, $f_n: X \to Y$ are continuous and $f: X \to Y$ be s.t. $f_n \to f$ uniformly then by uniform convergence limit theorem, $f$ is continuous.

