\noindent $\Rightarrow$ (i) Let, the given set is $X$.

Let, $B$ be a basis for a topology on $X$, which has $K$ elements.

Let, $B = \{O_1, O_2, O_3, \ldots, O_K\}$, $O_i \subseteq X$, $i = 1, 2, 3, \ldots, K$

Let, the topology, $B$ generates is $T$.

$\therefore$ For any $x_0 \in X$ \& $U \in T$, s.t $x_0 \in U$, we have $O_{i_0} \in B$, s.t $x_0 \in O_{i_0} \subseteq U$.

If we consider such $x_0$ from any open set $U \in T$, by above argument

Then $U$ can be written as, union of basis elements.

$\therefore$ The all open sets $B$ generates, those are generated by arbitrary union of subcollection of $B$.

Now, \sout{Anbity union of subcollection of $B$, will be} Some combination of union of elements $0, 0_2, \ldots, 0$.

So, every element $O_i$ in $B$ has two choice while union them, either it is in the subcollection or not in the subcollection.

Even, clearly, all these subcollections are actually elements of power set of $B$, which has $2^K$ elements.

$\therefore$ The topology $B$ generated by $B$ has at-most $2^K$ elements. (Proved)

