since $U \cap A = \{0\}$ \quad $\therefore$ $0 \in U$

$\Rightarrow$ $a < 0$ \quad $b > 0$ then $U = (a,b)$ \quad contains $\cap A$

contains points of the form $U \cap A = \{0\} \cup \left\{\frac{1}{n} \mid n \in \mathbb{N} \text{ and } \frac{1}{n} < b\right\}$

$\because$ By Archimedean property, $\forall \varepsilon > 0$, $\exists \, n \in \mathbb{N}$

s.t. $\frac{1}{n} < \varepsilon$ \quad Choose $\varepsilon = b$.

$\rightarrow$ Hence, $\{0\}$ is open in $(\mathbb{R}, P(X))$ but not in subspace topology induced by subspace topology $A$ induced by $\mathbb{R}$

