\noindent (a) \quad $(f \circ g)V = I$

\hspace{1em} $(g \circ f)V = I$

\noindent Since, $f \circ g$ is equal to identity, $g$ is the inverse of $f$.

\noindent Hence $f^{-1}$ can be obtained from $g$ through EROs or RED.

\noindent And $f$ is a linear $f \times m$ which implies that $g$ is also a linear function.

\vspace{1em}

\noindent (b) If $\beta = \{b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n\}$ is a basis for $V$, then $(t_1 b_1 + t_2 b_2 + \ldots + t_n b_n)$ can span the whole $V$.

\noindent Any linear operations on $\beta$ will lead to vectors which form the span of $V$ space.

\noindent So, $f(\beta) = \{f(b_1), f(b_2), \ldots, f(b_n)\}$ will be a linearly operated form of the basis of $V$

\noindent Hence,
\[ f(\beta) = \{f(b_1), f(b_2), \ldots, f(b_n)\} \text{ will give the linear combination as:} \]
\[ \left(t_1 f(b_1) + t_2 f(b_2) + \ldots + t_n f(b_n)\right) \]

\noindent It can span the whole basis $V$ and the number of elements is same as $\beta$.

\noindent Hence $f(\beta)$ is a basis for $V$.

