\]

$\rightarrow$ To show $S$ is subspace of $M_{4 \times 4}(\mathbb{R})$, we take two matrices $B, B^c \in S$, $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$

\[
B = \begin{bmatrix} a & b & c & a+b+c \\ d & e & f & d+e+f \\ g & h & i & g+h+i \\ j & k & l & j+k+l \end{bmatrix} - c = \begin{bmatrix} a' & b' & c' & a'+b'+c' \\ d' & e' & f' & d'+e'+f' \\ g' & h' & i' & g'+h'+i' \\ j' & k' & l' & j'+k'+l' \end{bmatrix}
\]

\[
B + c = \begin{bmatrix} a+a' & b+b' & c+c' & a+a'+b'+b+c+c' \\ d+d' & e+e' & f+f' & d+d'+e'+f'+f' \\ g+g' & h+h' & i+i' & g+g'+h+h'+i+i' \\ j+j' & k+k' & l+l' & j+j'+k+k'+l+l' \end{bmatrix}
\]

clearly $B + c \in S$ \quad $\therefore S$ is closed under addition.

\[
\alpha B = \begin{bmatrix} \alpha a & \alpha b & \alpha c & \alpha a+\alpha b+\alpha c \\ \alpha d & \alpha e & \alpha f & \alpha d+\alpha e+\alpha f \\ \alpha g & \alpha h & \alpha l & \alpha g+\alpha h+\alpha i \\ \alpha j & \alpha k & \alpha l & \alpha j+\alpha k+\alpha l \end{bmatrix} \quad \therefore \alpha B \in S
\]

$\therefore S$ is closed under scalar multiplication.

