\noindent (c) Given, two matrices
\[
M = \begin{bmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 4 \end{bmatrix} \quad \& \quad N = \begin{bmatrix} 4 & 1 \\ 0 & 3 \end{bmatrix}
\]

\noindent for two matrices are to be similar.

\noindent we will find their determinant \& eigenvalue must be same.

\[
\therefore \det(M) = |M| = \begin{vmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 4 \end{vmatrix} = 12
\]

\[
\det(N) = |N| = \begin{vmatrix} 4 & 1 \\ 0 & 3 \end{vmatrix} = 12
\]

\noindent and also
\[
\begin{aligned}
|A - \lambda I| &= 0 \\
2D \begin{vmatrix} M - \lambda I \end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\
2D \begin{vmatrix} 3-\lambda & 0 \\ 0 & 4-\lambda \end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\
2D (3-\lambda)(4-\lambda) &= 0 \\
\lambda &= 3, 4
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\begin{aligned}
|N - \lambda I| &= 0 \\
2D \begin{vmatrix} 4-\lambda & 1 \\ 0 & 3-\lambda \end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\
2D (4-\lambda)(3-\lambda) &= 0 \\
\lambda &= 4, 3
\end{aligned}
\]

\noindent $\therefore$ their eigenvalue and determinant are same so two matrices $M$ \& $N$ are similar.

