\noindent $\Rightarrow$ (i) Let, the given set is $X$.

Let, $B$ be a basis for a topology on $X$, which has $K$ elements.

Let, $B = \{O_1, O_2, O_3, \ldots, O_K\}$, $O_i \subseteq X$, $i = 1, 2, 3, \ldots, K$

Let, the topology, $B$ generates $\tau$.

$\therefore$ For any $x_0 \in X$ \& $U \in \tau$, s.t. $x_0 \in U$, we have $O_i \in B$, s.t. $x_0 \in O_i \subseteq U$.

If we consider such $x_0$ from any open set $U \in \tau$, by above argument

Then $U$ can be written as, union of basis elements.

$\therefore$ The all open sets $B$ generates, those are generated by arbitrary union of subcollection of $B$.

Now, An arbitrary union of subcollection of $B$, will be Some combination of union of elements $O_1, O_2, \ldots, O_k$.

So, every element $O_i$ in $B$ has two choice while union them, either it is in the subcollection or not in the subcollection.

Even, clearly, all these subcollection are actually elements of power set of $B$, which has $2^K$ elements.

$\therefore$ The topology is generated by $B$ has at-most $2^K$ elements. (Proved)

