\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent $E$ \& $E'$ be two systems in $m$ eqn of $n$ variables.

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent $\text{Span}(E) = \text{Span}(E')$

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent $\vec{E} = \begin{bmatrix} E_1 \\ \vdots \\ E_n \end{bmatrix} = \vec{U} + w\vec{W} + \cdots + aA = \begin{bmatrix} E_1' \\ E_2' \\ \vdots \\ E_n' \end{bmatrix} = \vec{E}$

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent (Now of variables $< n$)

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent Now, $w\vec{V} + w\vec{W} + \cdots + aA = (\vec{E} - \vec{U})$ \quad \textcircled{1}

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent Also, $w\vec{V} + w\vec{W} + \cdots + aA = (\vec{E}' - \vec{U})$ \quad \textcircled{2}

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent \underline{Correction 2} \quad \text{Methods}

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent Now, on solving, for solution for variables, the solutions will be same, i.e.

\noindent equation obtained for $\vec{E}$ and $\vec{E'}$ will similar, hence $E$ will be equivalent to $\vec{E'}$

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent \underline{Implication 2} \quad From eq(1) and eq(2),

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent We get
\[
\vec{E} - \vec{U} = \vec{E}' - \vec{U}
\]
\[
\vec{E} = \vec{E'}
\]

\noindent Hence, $\vec{E}$ will be equivalent to $\vec{E'}$

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent \underline{Proof}.

