Let $U$ be an open set in $(\mathbb{R}, P(X))$, let $U = \{0\}$

$\{0\}$ is open in $(\mathbb{R}, P(X))$ as singletons are open.

$\rightarrow$ But, $\exists \, U \subseteq$ subset of $X$ such that
\[
\left(\{0\} \cup \left\{\frac{1}{n} \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\right\}\right) \cap U = \{0\}
\]

$\because$ an open set $U$ of $\mathbb{R}$ is of the form $U = (a,b)$

since $U \cap A = \{0\}$ \quad $\therefore$ $0 \in U$

$\Rightarrow$ $a < 0$ \quad $b > 0$ then $U = (a,b)$ \quad $\cap A$ contains points of the form $U \cap A = \{0\} \cup \left\{\frac{1}{n} \mid n \in \mathbb{N} \text{ and } \frac{1}{n} < b\right\}$

$\because$ By Archimedean property, $\forall \varepsilon > 0$, $\exists \, n \in \mathbb{N}$ s.t. $\frac{1}{n} < \varepsilon$. Choose $\varepsilon = b$.

$\rightarrow$ Hence, $\{0\}$ is open in $(\mathbb{R}, P(X))$ but not in subspace topology induced by subspace topology $A$ induced by $\mathbb{R}$

