\noindent (i) Let $A$ be a $4 \times 4$ invertible upper triangular matrix.

\[
A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\ 4 & 1 & 3 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \Rightarrow |A| \neq 0 \Rightarrow \text{inverse exists.}
\]

\noindent w.k.t; $A^{-1} = \dfrac{\text{Adj}(A)}{|A|}$

\[
|A| = \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\ 4 & 1 & 3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} = 1[3(2)] = 6 \neq 0
\]
\[
\therefore |A| = 6
\]

\[
\text{Adj}(A) = \begin{bmatrix} 6 & -4 & -1 & -17 \\ -0 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -9 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \end{bmatrix} \Rightarrow \text{Adj}A = \begin{bmatrix} 6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ -4 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\ -1 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\ -17 & 1 & -9 & 6 \end{bmatrix}
\]

\noindent cofactor matrix of $A = \begin{bmatrix} 6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ -4 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\ -1 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\ -17 & 1 & -9 & 6 \end{bmatrix}$

\[
\therefore A^{-1} = \dfrac{\text{Adj}A}{|A|} = \dfrac{1}{6} \begin{bmatrix} 6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ -4 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\ -1 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\ -17 & 1 & -9 & 6 \end{bmatrix} \rightarrow \text{upper triangular matrix.}
\]

\noindent $\therefore$ Inverse of a $4 \times 4$ invertible upper triangular matrix is upper triangular. (similarly it obeys for lower triangular matrix as well).

\noindent This can also be shown by, a

\[
A = \begin{bmatrix} a & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ b & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ y & z & c & 0 \\ p & q & r & d \\ 4 |A| \neq 0 \end{bmatrix} \Rightarrow A^{-1} = \dfrac{\text{Adj}A}{|A|} = \begin{bmatrix} l & 0 & 0 & 0 \\ m & n & 0 & 0 \\ s & t & u & 0 \\ e & f & g \end{bmatrix} \rightarrow \text{upper triangular}
\]

