\[
(x, 1) \sim (x, 2) \quad \text { for   all } x \neq 0
\]

Let \( L = X / \sim \) be the quotient space, and let \( q: X \to L \) be the canonical quotient map. We denote the equivalence classes of the origins as \( 0_1 = [(0,1)] \) and \( 0_2 = [(0,2)] \).

(i) Show that \( L \) is locally homeomorphic to \( \mathbb{R} \) at every point.

(ii) Given any \(a, b \in L\) such that at least one of them is not equal to either \(0_1\) or \(0_2\), show that there exists disjoint open sets \(A, B \subset L\) containing \(a\) and \(b\) respectively.

(iii) Prove that any open set \( U \subset L \) containing \( 0_1 \) and any open set \( V \subset L \) containing \( 0_2 \) have a non-empty intersection \( (U \cap V \neq \emptyset) \). Conclude whether \( L \) is Hausdorff.

(iv) Is the quotient map \( q \) an open map? Justify your answer.

