\noindent 4A: Given,
\[ f: V \to V \text{ be a linear operator on a vector space } V. \]
It exists a function $g: V \to V$, such that $f \circ g$ and $g \circ f$ are identity on $V$.

\vspace{1em}

\noindent \textcircled{a}: \quad $f: V \to V \Rightarrow g: V \to V$.

\[ f \circ g = g \circ f = \bar{g} \quad \& \quad \bar{f} \]

\[ f \circ g = \bar{g}^{-1} \]

\[ g \circ f = \bar{f}^{-1} \]

\[ f \circ g = \bar{g}^{-1} = g \circ f = \bar{f}^{-1} = 0 \]

\vspace{1em}

\noindent So that;

\[ f \circ g = \bar{g}^{-1} = 0 \]

\[ g \circ f = \bar{f}^{-1} = 0 \]

\noindent if $\bar{g}^{-1} = 0$ then $f \circ g = 0$.

\noindent $\bar{f}^{-1} = 0$ then $g \circ f = 0$

\noindent So, $g = 0$ and $f = 0$.

\noindent So, we proved $g = 0$ is linear.

\noindent And $g$ is linear.

